\") and provides immediate, observable feedback. The simplified block structure removes unnecessary complexity, presenting a single user-friendly block for representing the \"IF-ELSE\" statement.\n\n\nAnalyzing Level 3 missions confirms the consistent use of a clear \"IF-THEN-ELSE\" structure. Each mission presents a single conditional rule involving two distinct outcomes based on a binary condition. This supports a model where simplified \"rule blocks\" with visual cues can replace the current complex nested structure. The goal now is to apply the structure to each new mission.\n\n\nEach Level 3 mission demonstrates a clear pattern: a single binary condition determining the box placement for missions 1-4. Mission 5 expands this to a nested conditional with three possible outcomes. The goal is to design a simplified block structure that encapsulates these patterns. The engine's `runSort` function and block processing need to be examined.\n\n\nThe proposed \"smart card\" visually represents the \"IF-ELSE\" structure as a single, easily configurable unit. This directly encodes the conditional logic (e.g., even/odd) and target destinations within a unified block. This visual approach aligns with the engine's expected `if` block format, automatically generating the underlying `type`, `cond`, `then`, and `else` properties. This design aims to make the conditional logic easily accessible.\n\n\nThe simplified \"rule block\" design is confirmed to be compatible with the core sorting engine and all calculation methods. The goal is now to create an intuitive block interface, keeping it free from nested complexity and suitable for children. A visual element, the \"Little Courier\" fox, will guide the sorting process with corresponding animations and sounds.\n\nThe objective is to implement the \"smart card\" in the `BlockEditor.tsx`, presenting a minimal interface for sort missions. The interface should focus either on relevant block options or directly offer a single rule card. The \"rule card\" will incorporate \"if-then-else\" logic, making Mission 5's two-level branching intuitive.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"e:\\\\codekids\\\\src\\\\components\\\\mission\\\\BlockEditor.tsx\"","EndLine":"100","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Viewing BlockEditor lines 1-100\"","toolSummary":"\"View BlockEditor lines 1-100\""}}]}
